    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change mirrors the existing `namesakeCount` precedent almost exactly \u2014 a value derived at creation time, stored as a nullable entity field with matching DB columns across all four dialects and a `readOnly` OpenAPI property, flowing to `OwnerDto` through the existing mapper. That's a well-established pattern in this codebase, so a maintainer would find it natural.\n\nStructurally:\n- `applyPossibleDuplicate` sits alongside its siblings (`rejectDuplicateIdentity`, `findHouseholdMembers`, `countOwnersInCity`, `countOwnersRegisteredOn`) and follows the same shape: stream `findAllOwners()`, filter with `normalizeForComparison` for case/whitespace-insensitive last-name comparison. It reuses the shared normalization helper rather than reinventing it.\n- It's invoked in the create pipeline right after `rejectDuplicateIdentity`, which is the correct ordering (the javadoc's \"already passed `rejectDuplicateIdentity`\" invariant holds) and the right unit \u2014 this is per-create business logic that belongs with the other create-time checks.\n- No new duplication: there was no existing lastName+postcode lookup to reuse (`findHouseholdMembers` keys on lastName+address), and the per-helper `findAllOwners()` streaming is the established idiom here, not a regression this change introduces.\n- The entity getters/setters and javadoc match the surrounding style, including the honest note about `null` for pre-existing owners.\n\nThe controller is already a hub of these create-time helpers, so this addition is consistent with \u2014 not an escalation of \u2014 the existing design.",
